==============================================================================
aws sts get-caller-identity
==============================================================================
The aws sts get-caller-identity command is the AWS equivalent of the Linux whoami command. It is a vital tool for verifying which AWS identity is currently being used to run commands, especially when managing multiple accounts or profiles. 

Why is it used to update information files?

In automated environments and scripts, this command is often used to "fill in the blanks" for configuration or information files because:
Verification: It confirms that your credentials are valid and active.
Dynamic ID Retrieval: It is the most reliable way to programmatically fetch your AWS Account ID without hardcoding it, which is essential for cross-account scripts.
Environment Context: It identifies exactly which IAM Role or User is active, preventing accidental deployments to the wrong account (e.g., using a Prod profile instead of Dev).
Zero Permissions Required: Uniquely, this command always works—it does not require any IAM permissions to run, making it the perfect first step for any script to "check in" with AWS. 

Understanding the Output

The command returns three critical pieces of information: 
Account: The 12-digit AWS Account ID where the credentials live.
Arn: The full "Amazon Resource Name" of the identity (e.g., arn:aws:iam::123456789012:user/YourName).
UserId: A unique internal identifier for the entity. 

