aws ec2 describe-images `
   --region ap-south-1 `
   --image-ids ami-051a31ab2f4d498f5
==============================================================================
This command is the "Deep Dive Inspection" for a specific AMI. While describe-images with filters is for searching, using it with a specific --image-ids is for verifying the exact details of a template you already found. AWS CLI describe-images Reference

What this specific command does:
Targeted Lookup: It bypasses the entire AWS library and pulls the metadata for only ami-051a31ab2f4d498f5.
Region Specific: It looks in Mumbai (ap-south-1). Remember, AMI IDs are unique to their region; this ID won't work in us-east-1. AWS AMI Regionality Guide
Detailed Metadata: It returns the "DNA" of that image, including:
--Architecture: (x86_64 or arm64/Graviton)
--Root Device Name: (e.g., /dev/xvda)
--Virtualization Type: (hvm)
--Creation Date: Exactly when this version was published.
--Block Device Mappings: The size and type of the EBS volume that will be created. Amazon Machine Images (AMI) Overview


==============================================================================
aws ec2 run-instances `
    --image-id ami-051a31ab2f4d498f5 `
    --instance-type t2.micro `
    --key-name MyKey `
    --security-group-ids sg-0c843cdfe4t4tg83 `
    --count 1
==============================================================================
This is the "Deploy" command—the moment your virtual server actually starts breathing in the Mumbai data center. AWS CLI run-instances Reference

What is happening behind the scenes?
Request Sent: Your CLI sends a request to the ap-south-1 endpoint.
Resource Allocation: AWS finds a physical host with enough CPU/RAM for a t2.micro.
Boot Process: It uses ami-051a31ab2f4d498f5 (the OS blueprint you found earlier) to create a root EBS volume. Amazon EC2 Instance Launching
Security Applied: It "wraps" the instance in sg-0c843cdfe4t4tg83, which controls who can talk to your server (e.g., SSH or Web traffic). Amazon EC2 Security Groups
Authentication: It injects the public half of MyKey into the instance so you can eventually log in via SSH. Amazon EC2 Key Pairs


